    10	# Design analysis for the combo:
    11	# a) Concrete behavior trace:
    12	#    Original e12f9a14 split collisions into two families. A cardinal beam meeting a diagonal
    13	#    beam at 45 degrees created a thicker zigzag, while a 90-degree collision created a blank
    14	#    meeting cell and then sent both beams along the bisector. In this combo, those branches
    15	#    disappear. Every collision is rewritten the same way: roll both beams back to the last
    16	#    uncontested cells, keep the contested location empty, then choose a legal 90-degree turn
    17	#    for each beam. Example: an east-going beam from a right-edge gap meets a NE beam from a
    18	#    top-right gap. The original task would turn them into a thick zigzag. Here the east beam
    19	#    bends upward, the NE beam bends rightward, and both remain single-pixel lines.
    20	# b) Complexity calibration:
    21	#    The rule is simpler, but not degenerate. The generator always includes at least one legacy
    22	#    45-degree collision, so the new output is visibly different from the original thick-zigzag
    23	#    behavior. Rotation, grid size, separation, and colors vary, while the collision grammar
    24	#    stays small enough for a human to infer from a few examples.
    25	# c) Hidden-assumption audit:
    26	#    The old solver assumed pattern/phase switching, 2x2 thickening, and angle-specific
    27	#    collision buckets. None of those remain valid. The new solver removes pattern cycling,
    28	#    never paints multi-cell-wide trajectories, and handles cell / diagonal-cross / segment-cross
    29	#    events through the same redirection primitive. The new assumption is local free space around
    30	#    the collision so two turned continuations can be chosen; the generator guarantees this by
    31	#    using a motif whose forced turn cells are clear.
    32	# d) Placement legality:
    33	#    The generator only stamps 4x4 framed blocks fully inside the grid, keeps them separated,
    34	#    and validates the finished grid by running the new solver. The collision motif computes
    35	#    its own runway so beams do not hit another frame before colliding or turning.
    36	# e) Visual observability:
    37	#    Each sample contains a deliberate old-45-degree collision. In the original puzzle family,
    38	#    that would create a thick zigzag. In this combo, the same setup yields two crisp single-pixel
    39	#    orthogonal deflections around an empty meeting point, which is easy to spot by eye.
    40	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    41	#    The generator uses only two framed blocks and one forced collision motif, so there is little
    42	#    overdraw. The blank collision cell keeps colors from competing for one pixel and preserves a
    43	#    readable bend for both beams.
    44	# g) Inferability of change:
    45	#    No hidden numeric ordering is used. Colors only identify which frame emitted which beam.
    46	#    The key clue is geometric: diagonal/cardinal beams that used to merge into thick paths now
    47	#    remain thin and peel off into orthogonal polylines.
